            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The inclusion of Gentiles into the covenant promises and people of God, sharing in the spiritual heritage and root of Israel.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Romans 11:17-24\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Individual spiritual union with Christ, drawing life from Him as the source (often conflating Paul's olive tree imagery with Jesus's vine imagery).\",\"basis\":[\"John 15:1-8\",\"Romans 11:17\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Replacement Theology (Supersessionism), interpreting the grafting of wild branches and the breaking off of natural branches as the Gentile Church permanently replacing ethnic Israel in God's plan.\",\"basis\":[\"Romans 11:19-20\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"A warning against Gentile pride, emphasizing that the Church remains dependent on the historical covenants made with Israel's patriarchs and that God is able to graft the natural branches back in.\",\"basis\":[\"Romans 11:18\",\"Romans 11:23-24\"],\"prominence\":\"dominant\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"Romans 11 is the primary and nearly exclusive biblical text for this specific symbolic phrasing. Paul utilizes the agricultural practice of grafting a wild olive branch into a cultivated olive tree to illustrate how Gentiles are brought into God's redemptive plan while affirming God's ongoing relationship with Israel.\"}",
